artifact.self_contained_problem:
A planetary nebula is the ionized circumstellar shell ejected by a low- to intermediate-mass star (initial mass roughly 1–8 solar masses) as it transitions from the asymptotic giant branch (AGB) to the white dwarf stage. Its evolution traditionally consists of: (i) a slow, dense, approximately spherical AGB wind (terminal speed ~10–20 km/s, mass-loss rates up to ~10^{-4} solar masses per year during the superwind); (ii) a proto-planetary-nebula (pre-PN) phase, during which the central star contracts and heats while the AGB envelope detaches; (iii) the planetary-nebula phase proper, when a fast (~10^3 km/s) tenuous post-AGB wind from the hot central star drives a shock into the previously ejected slow wind, and ionizing UV radiation lights up the nebula. Many observed planetary nebulae display non-spherical morphologies classified as bipolar (two opposed lobes separated by an equatorial waist), multipolar (multiple pairs of lobes), or point-symmetric (features symmetric under inversion through the central star).

Identify the dominant physical mechanism that first imprints departures from spherical symmetry on the circumstellar matter that later evolves into bipolar, multipolar, or point-symmetric planetary nebulae, and determine at which of the following evolutionary stages such asymmetries first emerge:
(a) already during the AGB superwind itself;
(b) during the proto-planetary-nebula phase, before the fast central-star wind switches on;
(c) only after the onset of the fast post-AGB wind interacting with the slow AGB wind via the generalized interacting-stellar-winds mechanism.

Candidate mechanisms to be evaluated include, but are not restricted to: rapid stellar rotation, large-scale magnetic fields anchored in the AGB envelope, single-star pulsation/convection-driven asymmetric mass loss, common-envelope evolution with a stellar or substellar companion, wind Roche-lobe overflow in a wide binary, accretion onto a companion launching collimated jets, and disk-mediated magnetohydrodynamic outflows. Provide the determination of the responsible process and the corresponding onset epoch.

